Core Architecture Components
1. Identity & Resource Hierarchy (Landing Zone)
Organization Node: Highest-level resource node representing the enterprise domain.
Folder Structure: Enforces policy separation by environment (Prod, Staging, Dev) or business unit.

Identity Federation: Integrated with existing IdP (Microsoft Entra ID, Okta) via Cloud Identity using SAML 2.0 / OIDC.  
IAM & Least Privilege: Access granted using Google Groups tied to predefined or custom IAM roles. 
Service accounts use short-lived credentials via IAM Workload Identity Federation.  

2. Network Topology & Edge SecurityShared VPC: Single host project controls subnets, firewall rules, and routes across attached service projects. 
Perimeter Security: Cloud Armor handles DDoS protection and WAF rules (OWASP Top 10) at the edge, integrated with Cloud Load Balancing.
Egress Traffic Control: Private workloads route internet traffic through Cloud NAT without public IPs assigned to compute resources.
Hybrid Connectivity: Cloud VPN (IPsec) or Cloud Interconnect links the enterprise on-premises data center directly to the Shared VPC.

3. Compute & Application HostingContainer Workloads: Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) Autopilot for microservices requiring orchestration, autoscaling, and private cluster configurations.
Serverless Services: Cloud Run for stateless API containers, deployed behind private service connectors.
Legacy / Stateful Compute: Compute Engine Managed Instance Groups (MIGs) configured across multiple availability zones with auto-healing enabled.  

4. Data Layer & StorageRelational Storage: Cloud SQL (HA multi-AZ pair) or Cloud Spanner for high-availability database requirements with automated backups.
Unstructured Storage: Cloud Storage (GCS) buckets with lifecycle rules (Standard -> Nearline -> Coldline) and CMEK (Customer-Managed Encryption Keys).
Event-Driven Stream Data: Cloud Pub/Sub decoupled messaging queue driving processing pipelines with Dataflow (Apache Beam).
Analytics & AI: BigQuery serving as the centralized enterprise data warehouse with direct access for Vertex AI models.

5. Security, Governance & ObservabilityKey Management: Cloud KMS manages lifecycle of customer-managed keys (CMEK) for at-rest data encryption.
Secrets Management: Secret Manager securely stores and rotates API keys, database credentials, and certs.Security Posture: Security Command Center (SCC) Premium scans for misconfigurations, compliance drifts, 
and active threats.
Centralized Logging & Monitoring: Cloud Logging aggregated sinks route logs to BigQuery for audits and SIEM integration, alongside metrics mapped in Cloud Monitoring dashboards. 
